lamine can coordinate soluble metal species and bind exposed surface sites during colloidal growth. It can therefore change how rapidly precursors react and how readily the resulting particles remain dispersed.","label":"General background, not the authors' interpretation","reference":{"doi":"10.1021/cm4000476","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/cm4000476","title":"Oleylamine in Nanoparticle Synthesis","authors":["Stefanos Mourdikoudis","Luis M. Liz-Marzán"],"year":2013,"metadata_response_sha256":"10145cffa0994fbf2433c60f1c7ca0580cda251d1fbe3d72e8e219b1849dace1","resolver_redirect_verified":true,"exact_DOI_metadata_verified":true,"paper_or_abstract_requested":false},"reagent_specific":true,"topic":"amine-surface"},{"id":"heatup-cluster","title":"Stable cluster intermediates can delay particle growth","summary":"In some indium phosphide routes, unusually stable molecular clusters accumulate instead of turning immediately into a smoothly growing distribution. Their appearance breaks the simple link between mixing the precursor and forming the final nanocrystals.","label":"General background, not the authors' interpretation","reference":{"doi":"10.1021/acs.chemmater.5b00286","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.chemmater.5b00286","title":"Two-Step Nucleation and Growth of InP Quantum Dots via Magic-Sized Cluster Intermediates","authors":["Dylan C. Gary","Maxwell W. Terban","Simon J. L. Billinge","Brandi M. Cossairt"],"year":2015,"metadata_response_sha256":"cc30b14a9dfbfab52c89494742d860ec1dc456a6a6be5224a6ff68bfe809cb66","resolver_redirect_verified":true,"exact_DOI_metadata_verified":true,"paper_or_abstract_requested":false},"reagent_specific":false,"topic":"magic-size-clusters"}]}
